    shortDescription: "REPORTED / SOURCE-LISTED | Intermediate. Source-based interview question; use the source classification as evidence level, not as a guarantee.",
    sampleAnswer: "Conversion-rate optimization (CRO) improves the percentage of\nrelevant visitors who complete a desired action. I would first identify\nthe conversion definition and funnel, analyze user behavior, locate\nmajor drop-off points, inspect page speed and usability, and form\nhypotheses.\n\nThen I would test changes such as clearer value propositions, stronger\ncalls to action, simpler forms, better product information, trust\nsignals, or improved mobile UX. Results should be measured with\ncontrolled experiments where feasible rather than assuming a change\ncaused improvement.",
    detailedAnswer: "Source classification: [REPORTED / SOURCE-LISTED]\n\nSource level: Intermediate\n\nAnswer:\nConversion-rate optimization (CRO) improves the percentage of\nrelevant visitors who complete a desired action. I would first identify\nthe conversion definition and funnel, analyze user behavior, locate\nmajor drop-off points, inspect page speed and usability, and form\nhypotheses.\n\nThen I would test changes such as clearer value propositions, stronger\ncalls to action, simpler forms, better product information, trust\nsignals, or improved mobile UX. Results should be measured with\ncontrolled experiments where feasible rather than assuming a change\ncaused improvement.\n\nExample:\nExample: If 1,000 qualified visitors produce 40 purchases,\nconversion rate is 4%. An A/B test might compare the current checkout\nwith a shorter version to determine whether friction is reducing\npurchases.\n\nInterview Tip:\nGive the definition first, then explain why it matters,\nprovide a practical example, and connect the concept to a measurable\nmarketing outcome.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nUnderstanding of digital-marketing\nfundamentals plus the ability to connect channels, metrics, customer\nintent, experimentation, and business outcomes.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\nTie the concept to audience intent, funnel\nstage, measurement, optimization, and business impact.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\nWhy would you choose this approach? What\nmetric would you use? What could cause the metric to be misleading? How\nwould you test your decision? What alternative would you consider?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\nFocusing only on clicks or impressions; confusing\nrelated metrics; assuming correlation proves causation; ignoring\ntracking quality; changing several variables at once; or giving a\nchannel recommendation without considering audience intent, economics,\nand business objectives.",
